# OneCoin / “Cryptoqueen” Fraud Scheme: Evidence-Based Reconstruction

**Status date: October 1, 2026**

## Executive summary

The returned evidence describes OneCoin as an international cryptocurrency investment fraud that began operations in Bulgaria in 2014 and was promoted through a global multi-level-marketing (MLM) network. The scheme marketed OneCoin as a cryptocurrency, recruited purchasers through members and distributors, and generated commissions for recruitment and sales. The U.S. Department of Justice describes OneCoin as a fraudulent cryptocurrency and states that victims invested more than $4 billion worldwide between 2014 and 2019. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

The principal founders identified in the returned sources are **Ruja Ignatova** and **Karl Sebastian Greenwood**. The DOJ states that they and others orchestrated the international investment scheme. Greenwood is also described as OneCoin’s top MLM distributor; he was sentenced to 20 years in prison and ordered to pay approximately $300,000 in forfeiture. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered); [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The sources support allegations that OneCoin’s purported value was set arbitrarily rather than by genuine market supply and demand, and that Greenwood claimed a private blockchain existed even though no publicly verifiable blockchain existed. The returned material does not supply technical evidence about any underlying database or software, so the narrower, source-supported conclusion is that the publicly verifiable blockchain claim was false or deceptive as described in the government’s case. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

Ruja Ignatova’s current status remains unresolved on the returned evidence. The sources establish that she is wanted by U.S. authorities, is described as an FBI Top Ten Fugitive, remains at large, and is being pursued by the FBI. They do **not** establish whether she is alive, dead, presumed dead, or currently located anywhere. Claims concerning her alleged murder, a yacht, or disposal of remains are unsupported by the returned sources and are therefore not included as established facts.

## Sources and evidentiary treatment

Only two source records were returned by the benchmark research:

- **[SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)** — U.S. Department of Justice, “Justice Department Announces Compensation Process for OneCoin Fraud Victims With Funds Recovered Through Asset Forfeiture,” April 13, 2026.
- **[SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)** — ArentFox Schiff, “Co-Founder of Fraudulent Cryptocurrency Sentenced to 20 Years in Prison,” September 15, 2023.

The sources differ in character:

- **SRC-001** is an official DOJ release and supports enforcement facts, including the description of OneCoin as fraudulent, the founders’ identities, the worldwide scale of the fraud, the forfeiture and compensation process, and Ignatova’s status as an FBI fugitive.
- **SRC-002** is a secondary legal analysis summarizing the government’s allegations and enforcement proceedings. Assertions concerning arbitrary valuation, the absence of a publicly verifiable blockchain, recruitment commissions, Greenwood’s alleged earnings, and the approximately 3.5 million investor figure should be attributed to the government’s case or to the secondary source rather than presented as independently established technical or financial findings.

No additional source IDs, machine-readable entity IDs, or exact benchmark relationship names were returned. Unsupported details have therefore been omitted.

## Origins and chronology

### Beginning of operations

OneCoin began operations in **2014** and was based in **Sofia, Bulgaria**. The DOJ identifies the relevant fraud period as **2014 through 2019**. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

The secondary source likewise places OneCoin’s beginning in Bulgaria in 2014 and states that it began operating in the United States around **2015**. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The returned sources do not provide a complete event-by-event chronology. In particular, they do not establish:

- the exact date on which OneCoin was incorporated or launched;
- the dates of particular product releases or conferences;
- the dates on which particular countries were entered;
- the date on which sales or recruitment ended;
- a detailed chronology of the founders’ movements or operational decisions.

Accordingly, the supported chronology is limited to the following:

1. OneCoin began operations in Bulgaria in 2014.
2. It operated in the United States around 2015.
3. The DOJ describes the relevant fraud period as 2014–2019.
4. By the time of the enforcement proceedings, U.S. authorities had characterized the enterprise as an international cryptocurrency investment fraud.

## Operating model

### Cryptocurrency investment presentation

OneCoin was marketed and sold as a cryptocurrency through a global MLM network. The DOJ describes it as a “fraudulent cryptocurrency” and as part of a large international cryptocurrency investment scheme. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

The DOJ states that the founders sold “a lie disguised as cryptocurrency” and that victims invested more than $4 billion worldwide. The DOJ also describes victims as having been misled by falsified statements and empty promises into investing in a financial scheme that would not pay out. These are official enforcement characterizations, not a technical description of a functioning cryptocurrency network. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

### Alleged manipulation of value

According to the government’s case as summarized by SRC-002, OneCoin represented that its value was based on market supply and demand. The government allegedly asserted that the value was actually set arbitrarily, without regard to market forces. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

This supports the following carefully limited conclusion:

- **Established enforcement characterization:** OneCoin was treated by the DOJ as a fraudulent cryptocurrency investment scheme.
- **Prosecutorial allegation summarized by a secondary source:** OneCoin’s stated market-based valuation was allegedly false and its value was allegedly set arbitrarily.
- **Not established by the returned material:** the exact formula, internal instructions, dates, personnel, or software used to set the value.

### Blockchain representation

Greenwood allegedly claimed that OneCoin had a private blockchain that identified OneCoins and recorded historical transactions. SRC-002 states that no publicly verifiable blockchain actually existed. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The evidence does not establish whether OneCoin maintained any private database, internal ledger, token-generation system, or other software. The supported proposition is narrower: the returned source says that the claimed private blockchain was not supported by a publicly verifiable blockchain.

## Promotion, recruitment, and MLM structure

### Global MLM network

OneCoin was marketed through a **global multi-level-marketing network**. Members received commissions for recruiting others to purchase cryptocurrency packages. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered); [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The supported recruitment model can be summarized as follows:

1. OneCoin was presented to prospective purchasers as a cryptocurrency investment.
2. Existing members promoted the opportunity to new participants.
3. Recruits purchased cryptocurrency packages.
4. Members received commissions for recruiting purchasers.
5. The network operated internationally and used MLM-style distribution rather than relying only on direct sales by a central company.

### Greenwood’s distribution role

SRC-002 describes Greenwood as **OneCoin’s top MLM distributor**. It further states that he allegedly earned 5% of monthly OneCoin sales, totaling more than $300 million. This figure is presented in the returned material as an allegation or government account summarized by the secondary source, not as an independently verified accounting finding. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

### Limits of the evidence concerning packages and hierarchy

The returned sources refer to cryptocurrency packages but do not describe:

- the names or prices of particular packages;
- the contents of any education materials;
- the number or names of membership levels;
- the precise commission schedule;
- a complete distributor hierarchy;
- recruitment genealogy;
- the identities of regional or national distributors;
- the locations of particular promotional events.

It would therefore be unsupported to reconstruct a more detailed sales ladder or organizational chart from the returned evidence.

## Geographic reach and scale

The DOJ describes OneCoin as an international scheme that defrauded investors “from around the globe.” It states that victims invested more than **$4 billion worldwide** between 2014 and 2019. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

SRC-002 states that the government charged Greenwood and others with securing more than $4 billion from approximately **3.5 million investors**. The 3.5 million figure appears in the secondary source and should not be attributed to the DOJ release. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The specific geographic facts supported by the returned sources are:

- Bulgaria was the base and location where operations began in 2014.
- OneCoin began operating in the United States around 2015.
- The scheme was international and reached investors around the globe.
- The sources do not provide a complete list of countries or investor totals by jurisdiction.

## Movement of funds and proceeds

### Investor funds

The DOJ states that victims invested more than $4 billion worldwide. SRC-002 describes the government’s case as involving more than $4 billion in investments secured through alleged misrepresentations. The wording differs, but both sources identify the same approximate scale. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered); [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

### Greenwood’s alleged earnings and forfeiture

The returned evidence states that:

- Greenwood allegedly earned 5% of monthly OneCoin sales, totaling more than $300 million. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)
- Greenwood was ordered to pay approximately $300,000 in forfeiture. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The two figures should not be conflated. The more-than-$300-million amount is described as alleged earnings, while the approximately-$300,000 amount is the individual forfeiture reported in the secondary source.

### Asset forfeiture and victim compensation

The DOJ states that it pursued criminal forfeiture of property derived from the fraud scheme. As of its April 13, 2026 announcement, more than **$40 million** in forfeited assets was available for victim compensation. The DOJ also announced a remission process for eligible victims who purchased OneCoin between 2014 and 2019. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

The DOJ identifies the following enforcement and administrative participants:

- the FBI and IRS-CI conducted the criminal fraud investigation;
- IRS-CI worked to trace illicit funds and recover them for victims;
- the DOJ’s Office of International Affairs provided significant assistance;
- the Criminal Division’s Money Laundering, Narcotics and Forfeiture Section manages the remission process;
- Kroll Settlement Administration LLC serves as the remission administrator.

The returned evidence does **not** provide:

- bank-account numbers;
- cryptocurrency wallet addresses;
- specific wire transfers;
- named shell companies;
- property-by-property tracing;
- countries through which funds allegedly moved;
- a complete laundering route;
- a full accounting of recovered versus unrecovered proceeds.

No such details can be added without further tool-supported evidence.

## Principal founders and central participants

### Ruja Ignatova

The returned sources identify Ruja Ignatova as a **co-founder of OneCoin Ltd.** The DOJ states that she and Greenwood, together with others, orchestrated the international cryptocurrency investment scheme. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered); [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The supported role description is therefore:

- co-founder of OneCoin Ltd.;
- central participant in the scheme according to the DOJ’s enforcement description;
- subject of an ongoing FBI pursuit;
- identified by the DOJ as an FBI Top Ten Fugitive.

The sources do not provide a detailed division of responsibilities between Ignatova and Greenwood, such as specific corporate, technical, marketing, financial, or managerial duties. Those details are omitted.

### Karl Sebastian Greenwood

Greenwood is identified as a co-founder of OneCoin Ltd. and as one of the people who, with Ignatova and others, orchestrated the scheme. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

SRC-002 additionally describes him as:

- a co-founder of OneCoin;
- OneCoin’s top MLM distributor;
- a person who allegedly earned 5% of monthly OneCoin sales, totaling more than $300 million;
- a defendant sentenced to 20 years in prison;
- a person ordered to pay approximately $300,000 in forfeiture. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The returned sources do not provide the precise sentencing date.

### Other participants

The DOJ and SRC-002 refer generally to Ignatova, Greenwood, and “others.” The returned excerpts do not identify or describe the roles of additional participants.

Although the searches sought information about **Konstantin Ignatov** and **Mark Scott**, the returned source excerpts did not provide source-supported facts about either person’s role. They are therefore not included as established participants in this reconstruction.

## Enforcement actions and legal consequences

The DOJ states that it filed a number of OneCoin-related prosecutions in the Southern District of New York and that several key figures had been sentenced. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

The clearest individual enforcement outcome in the returned evidence concerns Greenwood:

- 20-year prison sentence;
- approximately $300,000 forfeiture order. [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

The DOJ’s 2026 release concerns recovery and compensation rather than supplying a complete account of every prosecution or sentence. It states that more than $40 million in forfeited assets was available for victim compensation and that eligible victims could seek remission. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered)

## Ruja Ignatova’s status

### Established enforcement facts

The returned evidence supports the following statements:

- Ignatova is identified as a co-founder of OneCoin.
- She is described by the DOJ as an FBI Top Ten Fugitive.
- The secondary source states that she was added to the FBI’s Top Ten Most Wanted List in June 2022.
- The secondary source states that she remains at large.
- The DOJ states that the FBI continues to pursue her. [SRC-001](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered); [SRC-002](https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/co-founder-fraudulent-cryptocurrency-sentenced-20-years-prison)

### What the returned evidence does not establish

The returned sources do not establish:

- that Ignatova is alive;
- that Ignatova is dead;
- that she is legally or investigatively presumed dead;
- her current physical location;
- that she is residing in a particular country;
- that she was murdered;
- that she was last seen on a yacht;
- that her remains were disposed of;
- that any particular intelligence or investigative assessment regarding her death is reliable.

The most precise evidence-based formulation is:

> **Ruja Ignatova remains wanted and at large from the perspective of the cited enforcement sources, while her current location and biological status—alive, dead, or presumed dead—remain unconfirmed by the returned evidence.**

“Missing” may describe her disappearance in ordinary language, but the sources expressly support the narrower enforcement descriptions **“remains at large,” “FBI Top Ten Fugitive,”** and continued FBI pursuit. They do not support a definitive conclusion about her fate.

## Evidentiary distinctions and unresolved issues

### Facts stated by an official enforcement source

The DOJ directly supports:

- OneCoin’s characterization as a fraudulent cryptocurrency;
- the international scope of the scheme;
- the 2014–2019 fraud period;
- more than $4 billion invested worldwide;
- Ignatova and Greenwood as co-founders;
- their role, with others, in orchestrating the scheme;
- Ignatova’s FBI fugitive status;
- criminal forfeiture and victim compensation;
- more than $40 million in forfeited assets available for compensation as of April 13, 2026.

### Allegations or government-case descriptions summarized by a secondary source

SRC-002 supports, with attribution and qualification:

- the approximately 3.5 million investor figure;
- the claim that OneCoin’s value was allegedly set arbitrarily;
- the claim that no publicly verifiable blockchain existed;
- the MLM commission structure;
- Greenwood’s alleged 5% commission and more-than-$300-million earnings;
- Greenwood’s sentence and forfeiture.

### Unsupported or unresolved matters

The returned material does not support:

- a complete country-by-country map;
- a full distributor or recruitment hierarchy;
- detailed education-package contents;
- specific transaction or laundering routes;
- wallet addresses or bank accounts;
- the roles of additional named participants not described in the excerpts;
- a technical reconstruction of OneCoin’s internal systems;
- any conclusion that Ignatova is alive, dead, or presumed dead.

## Sources

[1] [Justice Department Announces Compensation Process for OneCoin Fraud Victims With Funds Recovered Through Asset Forfeiture](https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-compensation-process-onecoin-fraud-victims-funds-recovered) — **SRC-001**, U.S. Department of Justice, April 13, 2026.  
